<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      Layers Swift SDK Privacy Manifest

      Audited 2026-05-01 (Tier 9 privacy posture work).
      Re-audited 2026-06-10 (audit M3): added PurchaseHistory.
      Re-audited 2026-08-28 against the code as shipped in 3.2.13, after a
      customer's pre-submission audit found the declarations below did not
      match what the SDK does. Every entry is now tied to the code that
      produces it — keep it that way.

      WHAT THIS SDK DOES WITH THE DATA (so the app can declare honestly):

      Every event carries a persistent per-install `device_id`,
      `anonymous_id`, `install_id`, the IDFV, and — once App Tracking
      Transparency has been authorized — the IDFA (core/src/client.rs,
      `build_event`). After `identify(userId:)` every event also carries the
      app's user id. When the host app connects an ad-network Conversions
      API in Layers (Meta, TikTok, OpenAI, LinkedIn), the Layers server
      forwards conversion events with those identifiers — the IDFA as
      Meta's `madid`, the install id as `anon_id`, the user id hashed as
      `external_id` — so the ad network can attribute the conversion to a
      campaign. Under Apple's definition that is tracking, and the data
      types below are LINKED to a persistent device identity and, after
      identify(), to the user.

      THE HOST APP MUST ANSWER "YES" TO "DO YOU OR YOUR THIRD-PARTY PARTNERS
      USE DATA FOR TRACKING?" IN APP STORE CONNECT WHEN AD-NETWORK
      FORWARDING IS ENABLED, and declare Device ID, Product Interaction,
      Purchase History, User ID and Advertising Data as linked to the user
      and used for tracking. https://layers.com/docs/sdk/app-store-privacy
      carries the copy-paste table.

      WHY NSPrivacyTracking IS STILL false HERE: Apple requires at least one
      domain in NSPrivacyTrackingDomains when NSPrivacyTracking is true, and
      iOS then fails every request to those domains for any user who has
      not granted ATT. This SDK has ONE host, in.layers.com, which carries
      first-party analytics and the ad-forwarded events alike, so listing
      it would silently drop ALL delivery for every non-authorized user.
      The honest flag needs a dedicated tracking host (tr.layers.com) that
      receives only the ad-forwarded traffic; that split is scheduled, and
      this flag flips with it. Until then the per-type Tracking flags stay
      in step with NSPrivacyTracking, and the obligation above is the app's.

      NOT collected by this SDK, and deliberately absent below:
      - Crash data: ExceptionModule.swift exists in this package but nothing
        in Layers.swift instantiates it. No handler is installed; no
        `$exception` event is ever emitted by the Swift wrapper.
      - Performance data: MetricKitModule.swift likewise is never
        constructed; no MXMetricManager subscription exists.
      - Precise or coarse location: the SDK reads no location API. (The
        Layers server resolves the connection IP to a coarse region for ad
        matching; that is a server-side derivation the app may need to
        declare — see the docs page above.)
    -->

    <key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
    <false/>
    <key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
    <array/>

    <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
    <array>
        <!-- device_id (persistent per-install UUID), anonymous_id,
             install_id, IDFV always; IDFA after ATT authorization
             (ATTModule.swift → core build_event). -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeDeviceID</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
            <true/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
            <false/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
            <array>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeThirdPartyAdvertising</string>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeDeveloperAdvertising</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
        <!-- app_user_id / user_id after identify(userId:), stamped on every
             subsequent event and forwarded hashed as external_id. -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeUserID</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
            <true/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
            <false/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
            <array>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeThirdPartyAdvertising</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
        <!-- Events, screens (automatic UIKit swizzle), sessions, lifecycle. -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeProductInteraction</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
            <true/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
            <false/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
            <array>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeThirdPartyAdvertising</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
        <!-- trackPurchase / CommerceModule (StoreKit auto-capture is OFF by
             default) and the RevenueCat / Superwall bridges: revenue,
             currency, product_id, transaction_id. -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurchaseHistory</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
            <true/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
            <false/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
            <array>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeThirdPartyAdvertising</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
        <!-- Click identifiers and campaign parameters captured for
             attribution (fbclid, gclid, ttclid, msclkid, li_fat_id, UTMs,
             the AdServices token) and forwarded to the matching network. -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeAdvertisingData</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
            <true/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
            <false/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
            <array>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeThirdPartyAdvertising</string>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeDeveloperAdvertising</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
    </array>

    <!--
      Required-reason APIs we touch.

      Audited usages:
      - UserDefaults: Swift wrapper persists `first_open` flag and small
        config hints for the host app's lifecycle observer.
        Reason CA92.1 — "Read or write to user defaults that are
        accessible only to the app itself or to a group that the app is
        a member of."
      - File timestamp APIs: the Rust persistence layer (FilePersistence
        in `core/src/persistence.rs`) writes timestamped queue files via
        `chrono::Utc::now().timestamp_millis()` and reads file metadata
        for hydration / TTL eviction. Atomic writes use `.tmp` + rename.
        Reason C617.1 — "Inside app container, app group container, or
        the app's CloudKit container."
      - System boot time: the Rust core's `Instant` type uses the
        platform clock to compute relative durations for `time_event()`,
        circuit-breaker reset timers, retry backoff, etc. On Apple
        platforms `std::time::Instant` reads the system boot time.
        Reason 35F9.1 — "Measure the amount of time that has elapsed
        between events that occur within the app."
      - Disk space: not currently used by the Rust core or Swift
        wrapper. Declaration intentionally omitted; if a future tier
        adds disk-space-aware persistence, add it here.
    -->
    <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
    <array>
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
            <array>
                <string>CA92.1</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
            <array>
                <string>C617.1</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategorySystemBootTime</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
            <array>
                <string>35F9.1</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
